你要逮捕的人。现在，谁来负责抓捕？福特将军认为德里的军队太被动了，当然。所以作为抓捕部队，他派出了他所拥有的最强硬、最具侵略性的人。这就是威尔福德中校的伞兵团第一营。现在，他们是最强硬的人。威尔福德本人实际上是一个非常了不起的人，竟然能指挥这样的人。惊人的故事。所以他来自，我想是莱斯特郡。

54
00:25:17.000 --> 00:25:39.000
He was born to a single mother. He was brought up by his grandparents and an anonymous donor paid for him to go to Loughborough Grammar School. At Loughborough Grammar School, he turned out to be an absolutely brilliant classicist and actor. So he was given a place at Rada, the Royal Academy of Dramatic Arts, where all the sort of top lovers go.
他由单身母亲抚养长大。他由祖父母抚养，一位匿名捐赠者支付了他去拉夫堡文法学校（Loughborough Grammar School）的费用。在拉夫堡文法学校，他证明自己是一个绝对出色的古典学者和演员。所以他被皇家戏剧艺术学院（RADA）录取了，那是所有顶级演员去的地方。

55
00:25:39.000 --> 00:25:58.000
He turned it down and ended up joining the army. His idea of relaxation is to read Virgil and Marcus Aurelius in the original, in Latin. It's a very first world war in the trenches. Very much, although less First World War, after he left the army, I was reading obituous of him today, and this blew my mind.
他拒绝了，最终加入了军队。他放松的方式是阅读拉丁文原版的维吉尔和马可·奥勒留。这非常像第一次世界大战在战壕里。非常像，虽然不太像第一次世界大战，在他离开军队后，我今天在读他的讣告，这让我大吃一惊。

56
00:25:58.000 --> 00:26:26.000
After he left the army, with perhaps a slightly check of reputation, he grew a ponytail and became a Belgian antiques dealer. It's an unexpected development. It's a massively unexpected development. Anyway, in 1972, this bloke commands one of the most feared battalions in the British army. They are ultra-professional, ultra-aggressive. He always has his men kind of blacking up with their faces and wearing camouflage. He says, you know, we're fighters and killers.
在他离开军队后，名声可能稍微有点受损，他留了马尾辫，成为了一名比利时古董商。这是一个意想不到的发展。这是一个非常意想不到的发展。无论如何，在1972年，这个家伙指挥着英国军队中最令人恐惧的营之一。他们超级专业，超级激进。他总是让他的手下把脸涂黑，穿着迷彩服。他说，你知道，我们是战士和杀手。

57
00:26:26.000 --> 00:26:43.000
We're not messing around here. What kind of camouflage are you wearing in Derry? You kind of dress up in, it looked like concrete or something. Dress as a tower block. A ballard. I don't know. I think you're just wearing it in case you suddenly sent to Malaya at a moment's notice. Well, anyway, he asked himself what would Marcus Aurelius do?
我们不是在这里胡闹。你在德里穿什么样的迷彩服？你打扮成混凝土之类的东西。打扮成塔楼。一个系船柱。我不知道。我想你只是穿着它，以防你突然被派往马来亚。好吧，无论如何，他问自己马可·奥勒留会怎么做？

58
00:26:43.000 --> 00:27:08.000
Marcus Aurelius would be wearing green combat fatigues, no? No, he wouldn't. He'd be dressing up as bollards. Fooling the rioters. Pouting. So even before Bloody Sunday, the Paris, these paras had a reputation, shall we say. So a few days beforehand, the then young journalist Simon Hoggart called them in print, they are the troops most hated by Catholics and troubled areas.
马可·奥勒留会穿着绿色战斗服，不是吗？不，他不会。他会打扮成系船柱。愚弄暴徒。撅着嘴。所以即使在“血腥星期日”之前，伞兵，这些伞兵就有名声，我们可以这么说。所以几天前，当时的年轻记者西蒙·霍加特（Simon Hoggart）在印刷品中称他们为“天主教徒和动荡地区最憎恨的部队”。

59
00:27:08.000 --> 00:27:46.000
And he said other British officers regard them as little better than thugs in uniform. And actually other officers thought it was a great mistake to send in the Paris. And in Peter Taylor's book Brits, he quotes officers who said, at the time it is mad to be using them to police a non-violent civil rights march. But of course, Ford wants to use the power as precisely because he thinks it will be violent. The atmosphere in Derry is very tense. Just three days before the march, the IRA had ambushed an RUC patrol car and they'd killed two policemen. And Ford's officers on the ground say to him,
他说其他英国军官认为他们不过是穿着制服的暴徒。实际上其他军官认为派伞兵进去是一个巨大的错误。在彼得·泰勒的书《英国人》中，他引用了当时军官的话说，用他们来维持非暴力民权游行的治安是疯狂的。但当然，福特想使用伞兵正是因为他认为这会变得暴力。德里的气氛非常紧张。就在游行前三天，爱尔兰共和军伏击了一辆皇家阿尔斯特警队巡逻车，打死了两名警察。福特在现场的军官对他说，

60
00:27:46.000 --> 00:28:16.000
look, this march may start peacefully. but the dairy young hooligans backed up by the gunmen, this is a quote, the derry young hooligans backed up by the gunmen will undoubtedly take control at an early stage. And what they're particularly worried about, this may actually remind people a very unlikely parallel, which is the German invasion of Belgium in 1914. What they're really worried about is snipers, you know, people shooting from roofs and from sort of behind barricades and things.
看，这场游行可能开始时是和平的。但由枪手支持的“德里年轻流氓”，这是一个引用，“由枪手支持的德里年轻流氓无疑会在早期阶段控制局面。”他们特别担心的是，这实际上可能会让人们想起一个非常不可能的相似之处，即1914年德国入侵比利时。他们真正担心的是狙击手，你知道，从屋顶和路障后面射击的人等等。

61
00:28:16.000 --> 00:28:51.000
The Paris operational orders explicitly wore, warn them. You should expect IRA terrorist activity. Almost certainly, snipers, petrol bombers and nail bombers will support the rioters. And snipers are their biggest fear. Snipers had killed 24 soldiers in the course of 1971. So when you, if you're a para and you wake up first thing in the morning, probably the threat of the sniper is the first thing you think of. But the parrars have the reputation they do because they are gung-ho, because they're not frightened. So one of them later remembers his commanding officer's words the night before. Let's teach them a lesson.
伞兵的作战命令明确警告他们。你应该预料到爱尔兰共和军的恐怖活动。几乎可以肯定，狙击手、汽油弹投掷者和钉子炸弹投掷者将支持暴徒。狙击手是他们最大的恐惧。狙击手在1971年杀死了24名士兵。所以当你，如果你是一名伞兵，你一大早醒来，狙击手的威胁可能是你首先想到的事情。但伞兵之所以有这样的名声，是因为他们好战，因为他们不害怕。所以其中一个人后来记得他指挥官前一天晚上的话。让我们给他们一个教训。

62
00:28:51.000 --> 00:29:17.000
We want some kills tomorrow. Anyway, so we get to the day itself. When you dig into the Savile inquiry, you know, unless you've got like 10 years to spend reading this stuff, it's daunting the amount of detail, minute by minute detail. But actually, the big picture of it is pretty straightforward. Lunchtime the marches assemble. And they set off just before 3 o'clock in the afternoon. There were about 12,000 of them.
我们明天想要一些战果。无论如何，我们来到了这一天。当你深入研究萨维尔调查时，你知道，除非你有10年的时间来阅读这些东西，否则细节的量是令人望而生畏的，逐分钟的细节。但实际上，大局是非常直接的。午餐时间游行者集合。他们在下午3点前出发。大约有12000人。

63
00:29:17.000 --> 00:29:44.000
And the atmosphere, contrary to expectation at first, is pretty relaxed, pretty upbeat. Actually, there were people at the time said it was almost like a carnival atmosphere. People were quite jolly. They set off. By half past three, they're approaching the city centre. It is, of course, blocked off, sealed off by an army barricade on William Street. So at that point, most of the marches turned right going towards the bogside. They're going to stop when they get to free dairy corner.
气氛，与最初的预期相反，相当轻松，相当乐观。实际上，当时有人说这几乎像狂欢节的气氛。人们相当快乐。他们出发了。到三点半，他们接近市中心。当然，它被威廉街上的军队路障封锁、密封了。所以在那一点上，大多数游行者向右转，走向博格赛德。他们到达自由德里角时会停下来。

64
00:29:44.000 --> 00:30:10.000
as it's called, the entrance to hear speeches, people denouncing internment. Now back at William Street by the barricade, blocking off the city centre, there are some scuffles between rioters and the paras. Teenagers basically throwing stones at the British Army. And this is where the first shots are fired. The paratroopers fire five rounds, and they wound a teenage boy and a 59-year-old man. But they don't kill anybody.
正如人们所称的那样，这是通往演讲现场的入口，人们在那里谴责拘留政策。现在回到威廉街的路障处，这里封锁了市中心，暴徒和伞兵之间发生了一些冲突。基本上是青少年在向英国军队投掷石块。就在这时，第一声枪响了。伞兵开了五枪，打伤了一名十几岁的男孩和一名59岁的男子。但他们没有杀死任何人。

65
00:30:10.000 --> 00:30:36.000
But that is what they've basically in order to do. to wound to wound hooligans and rioters. And a few minutes later, there is a sniper shot. So he's an official IRA man. He fires a single shot. He misses the soldiers and he hits a drain pipe. So this is not long before 4 o'clock. A 355, Lieutenant Colonel Wilford asks permission from the commanding officer in Derry.
但这基本上就是他们被命令要做的事：打伤那些流氓和暴徒。几分钟后，传来了一声狙击手的枪响。那是一名官方爱尔兰共和军成员。他开了一枪，没打中士兵，而是打中了一根排水管。所以这发生在下午4点之前不久。3点55分，中校威尔福德向德里的指挥官请求许可。

66
00:30:36.000 --> 00:30:51.000
He says, I wonder, can we send in the Paris to pick up yobbos? That's his phrase. and 12 minutes pass and then he gets the confirmation from the overall dairy commander as follows yes fine send in a para subunit
他说：“我想，我们能不能派伞兵去抓那些混混？”这是他的原话。12分钟过去了，他得到了德里总指挥官的确认，内容如下：“是的，可以，派出一支伞兵分队。”

67
00:30:51.000 --> 00:31:02.000
to do scoop up op through barrier 14 not to conduct running battle down Rossville Street now this last sentence is totally ignored because a few minutes later
“去清理14号路障，而不是在罗斯维尔街进行追逐战。”现在，这最后一句被完全忽略了，因为几分钟后，

68
00:31:02.000 --> 00:31:33.000
a convoy of armoured personnel carriers come screaming down the road down Rossville Street into the box side and paratroopers jump out and they immediately start grabbing people from the crowd, hitting them with kind of rifle butts, firing rubber bullets at this stage at them, and so on and so forth. And it's a scene, you've seen it so many times in this story in this series, the story of the troubles, chaos, hundreds of people screaming, people running away in panic, some people taking refuge
一队装甲运兵车尖叫着冲下街道，冲进博格赛德区，伞兵们跳下车，立即开始从人群中抓人，用步枪枪托殴打他们，在这个阶段还向他们发射橡胶子弹，诸如此类。这是一个场景，你在本系列关于“动乱”的故事中已经看过很多次了：混乱，数百人尖叫，人们惊慌失措地逃跑，有些人躲避

69
00:31:33.000 --> 00:32:06.000
behind rubble barricades, throwing stones and things, but most people just running away. And the paras claimed later that it was at this point that they came under fire from snipers on top of the high-rise flats overlooking the scene. This, I think, the self-halt inquiry established beyond any possible doubt is just not true. There were no witnesses that saw snipers. There were no guns found. There were no bullets found afterwards. There was nothing. What effectively happens in the next few minutes is that the paratroopers completely lose control.
在瓦砾路障后面，投掷石块等杂物，但大多数人只是在逃跑。伞兵后来声称，正是在这一点上，他们遭到了俯瞰现场的高层公寓楼顶上的狙击手的射击。我认为，萨维尔调查已经毫无疑问地证实，这根本不是真的。没有目击者看到狙击手。没有发现枪支。事后也没有发现子弹。什么都没有。在接下来的几分钟里，实际上发生的是伞兵完全失去了控制。

70
00:32:06.000 --> 00:32:27.000
I mean, it's not an unfamiliar story with people policing crowds. You know, it's a terrible story, but it's not unknown. Well, I suppose, I mean, the Vietnam comparison that Ted Kennedy made, is that kind of mili or something? Yeah. It's soldiers, frazzled on the edge, commit atrocities. Yeah. I mean, this is the funny thing about it.
我的意思是，这对于维持人群秩序的人来说并不是一个陌生的故事。你知道，这是一个可怕的故事，但并非闻所未闻。嗯，我想，泰德·肯尼迪所做的越南对比，那是某种军事上的吗？是的。这是士兵们在压力下崩溃，犯下暴行。是的，我的意思是，这就是关于这件事最讽刺的地方。

71
00:32:27.000 --> 00:32:50.000
It costs 200 million pounds to do this inquiry. But I remember reading, you know, I was researching my book on the early 70s at that point when the report came. And I thought, you know, this is exactly as I thought it always would be. This would be the outcome. Because psychologically, from everything we know about how people behave in these situations, this makes complete sense. They sort of panic. They're over pumped up, as it were.
这项调查耗资2亿英镑。但我记得读过，你知道，当报告出来时，我正在研究关于70年代初的书。我想，你知道，这完全是我一直认为会有的结果。因为从心理学上讲，根据我们所知的人们在这种情况下如何表现的一切，这是完全合理的。他们有点恐慌。他们被过度刺激了，可以这么说。

72
00:32:50.000 --> 00:33:07.000
They're very hard men. They're trained killers. They're also very young men. They lose control. They start shooting. They fire more than 100 rounds into the crowd. They hit 26 people and they kill 13 people. and most of the people they kill are very young.
他们是硬汉，是训练有素的杀手。他们也是非常年轻的人。他们失去了控制。他们开始射击。他们向人群发射了100多发子弹。他们击中了26人，杀死了13人。而且他们杀死的大多数人非常年轻。

73
00:33:07.000 --> 00:33:34.000
Six of them of the 13 were 17 years old. Most of the people they kill were running away, running to safety. Some of them were trying to help the injured, but we know that a lot of them were shot in the back. And the most famous image of the day is a Catholic priest called Edward Daly, and you can see the photos online. It's one of the ones that comes up immediately if you Google Bloody Sunday. It's a priest, and he's waving a blood-stained handkerchief as a white flag.
13人中有6人是17岁。他们杀死的大多数人当时正在逃跑，奔向安全地带。有些人试图帮助伤者，但我们知道他们中有许多人是背部中弹。那天最著名的照片是一位名叫爱德华·戴利的罗马天主教神父，你可以在网上看到这些照片。如果你谷歌“血腥星期日”，它会立即跳出来。照片里是一位神父，他挥舞着沾满血迹的手帕作为白旗。

74
00:33:34.000 --> 00:34:07.000
and they're trying to carry this guy to safety, 17-year-old Jackie Duddy. And Duddy dies just moments later. The priest, Edward Daly, was holding his hand and giving him the last rights as he died. And as you said, why this all happened is quite a straightforward. I think question to answer. If you send trained killers, renowned for their aggression, to police a march. And if you tell them, you're going to be attacked by snipers, you're making it quite likely that they will lose control.
他们正试图把这个家伙抬到安全地带，17岁的杰基·杜迪。杜迪在几分钟后就去世了。神父爱德华·戴利握着他的手，在他临终时为他做了临终祈祷。正如你所说，这一切为什么会发生，我认为是一个相当直截了当的问题。如果你派遣以侵略性著称的训练有素的杀手去维持游行秩序，并且如果你告诉他们“你们会遭到狙击手的袭击”，那么他们失去控制的可能性就非常大。

75
00:34:07.000 --> 00:34:27.000
So this is, I mean, General Ford had actually said it himself. That's why I read the quotation out. He'd said it in December. The risk of casualties is high. So-called unarmed rioters possibly teenagers are certain to be shot. Much will be made of the invasion of dairy and the slaughter of the innocent. Now, did the Paris feel guilty after this had happened? The short answer is no.
所以，我的意思是，福特将军自己其实也说过。这就是我读出那段引文的原因。他在12月就说过。伤亡风险很高。所谓的非武装暴徒，可能是青少年，肯定会被射杀。关于入侵德里和屠杀无辜者的言论将会很多。那么，伞兵在事后感到内疚吗？简短的回答是：没有。

76
00:34:27.000 --> 00:34:46.000
One of them says in the Brits that I mentioned, he says, we saw it as a job well done. I mean, this is possibly a heretical question. Yeah. Bearing in mind the military situation, was it a job well done? In other words, did it serve as a salutary atrocity
其中一个人在提到的英国人中说，我们认为这是一项完成得很好的工作。我的意思是，这可能是一个异端的问题。是的。考虑到当时的军事局势，这是一项完成得很好的工作吗？换句话说，它是否起到了一种警示性的暴行作用，

77
00:34:46.000 --> 00:35:32.000
which made people think twice? No, absolutely not. If you're going to take such a cold-blooded view of it, which I mean, it's not a fair question to ask, right? Yeah, it's the rich of the third, you know, they did it and they right to do it kind of thing. Absolutely not, because, I mean, the evidence is in what happens in the next 11 months in Northern Ireland, and indeed in the next 20-odd years, which is things got a lot worse, and it complete, as we'll see, it completely destroys the reputation of the British Army and corrods beyond measure the relationship between them and much of the local population. So absolutely not. If you were taking the most Machiavellian kind of possibly or Klausovitzian interpretation, then no, absolutely not at all.
让人们三思而后行？不，绝对不是。如果你要采取如此冷血的观点，我的意思是，这不是一个公平的问题，对吧？是的，这是那种“他们做了，他们做得对”之类的说法。绝对不是，因为证据就在接下来的11个月里北爱尔兰发生的事情中，实际上在接下来的20多年里也是如此，那就是情况变得更糟了，正如我们将看到的，它彻底摧毁了英国军队的声誉，并极大地腐蚀了他们与当地大部分民众之间的关系。所以绝对不是。如果你采取最马基雅维利式或克劳塞维茨式的解释，那么不，绝对不是。

78
00:35:32.000 --> 00:35:46.000
And what happens to free dairy? Still there. Do they not go in and bulldoze it? No, not at this point. No. That happens much later, think of Operation Moteman, but not at this point. No. Just on the Paris, actually, the job well done thing.
那么“自由德里”发生了什么？还在那里。他们没有进去推平它吗？不，不是在这个时候。不。那发生在很久以后，想想“莫特曼行动”，但不是现在。不。只是关于伞兵，关于“工作完成得很好”那件事。

79
00:35:46.000 --> 00:36:21.000
I guess to the end of their lives, a lot of the powers, I mean, they maintained and presumably still maintain that they did the right thing. They were shooting in self-defense. And actually, a really interesting person who's written about this, a very unexpected person is the extremely right-wing journalist. Douglas Murray. So Douglas Murray went, you know, assistant editor of the spectator or associate editor of spectator. He went to the Saville Inquiry and wrote a book about it. And he is incredibly scathing about the paratroopers. He calls them unrelenting liars. He says, you know,
我想直到他们生命的尽头，许多伞兵，我的意思是，他们坚持并且大概仍然坚持他们做了正确的事。他们是在自卫射击。实际上，一个写过这件事的非常有趣的人，一个非常出人意料的人，是极右翼记者道格拉斯·默里。所以道格拉斯·默里，你知道，《旁观者》的助理编辑或副编辑。他去了萨维尔调查现场并写了一本书。他对伞兵的评价极其尖刻。他称他们为“无情的骗子”。他说，你知道，

80
